      "math_academy_id": "q-11841",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=x\\cdot {3}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={3}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot {3}^{x}+x\\cdot ({3}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+(x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x}) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\  & =(1+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
